The '''early Kingdom of Galwa''' (1709–1750) refers to the formative period of the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa|western Burawi kingdom]] following the [[Burawa Imperial Split|Imperial Split]] of 1709. The period encompasses the regency of '''[[Sahra]]''' (1709–1740), the accession and early reign of '''[[Cali IV]]''' (1740–1750), the [[Bindorama Civil War]] (1735–1742), and the creation of the [[Parliament of Lords (Galwa)|Parliament of Lords]].

The Kingdom of Galwa emerged from the split as one of two successor states to the unified Burawi monarchy. Ruled initially by the child king Cali IV under his mother's regency, the western kingdom established its capital at [[Hargiesa]], developed an absolutist system of governance distinct from the parliamentarism of the eastern [[Kingdom of Burawa]], and navigated complex relationships with the [[Burawi Catholic Church]], the sister kingdom, and its colonial protectorate of [[Bindorama]]. The era concluded with Cali IV's creation of the Parliament of Lords, a reform that introduced limited representative government to Galwa.

== Background ==

{{main|Burawa Imperial Split}}

The [[Burawa Imperial Split]] of 1709 divided the unified Burawi kingdom into two successor states: the eastern [[Kingdom of Burawa]] under '''[[Iacob V]]''', and the western '''[[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]]''' under the child king '''[[Cali IV]]''' with his mother '''[[Sahra]]''' serving as regent. The split resolved a succession crisis that had threatened to plunge the realm into civil war and invite foreign intervention.

The division followed geographic boundaries: the [[Dimar River]] from its mouth to the [[Great Kaftian Lake]], then north along the lake to the [[Kitale River]], and thence to the sea. The western kingdom received the provinces of '''[[Galwa]]''', '''[[Fayeha]]''', and '''[[Wolonia]]''', along with the colonial protectorate of '''[[Bindorama]]''' in [[Abyala]].

The [[Burawi Catholic Church]], which had initially supported the Galwa claim during the succession crisis, shifted to a neutral position to facilitate the negotiated settlement. The [[Cardinal of Burawa]], formally transferred from [[Samara]] to [[Weli Markos]] as part of the reconciliation, retained ecclesiastical authority over both kingdoms and served as intermediary between them.

== Regency of Sahra (1709–1740) ==

=== Establishment of the Galwa court ===

Upon the formalization of the split in 1709, Sahra established the Galwa court at '''[[Hargiesa]]''', a city on the western coast that had served as an administrative centre for the western provinces during the unified period. The choice of Hargiesa over older centres such as the shores of the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] reflected both strategic considerations — distance from the disputed border with Burawa — and the western kingdom's orientation toward the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] and the Atlantic trade routes.

Sahra's regency consolidated the institutions of the new kingdom. The royal administration, drawn from officials who had served the unified crown and remained loyal to the Galwa faction, established ministries for finance, defence, colonial affairs, and ecclesiastical relations. The '''Council of Regents''', a body of senior nobles and churchmen appointed by Sahra, advised the regent and exercised delegated royal authority in matters of state.

=== Absolutist governance ===

Unlike the eastern kingdom, where [[Iacob V]] had been compelled to create a four-house parliament to secure his accession, the Galwa regency maintained absolutist governance. Sahra, exercising power on behalf of the minor king, governed through royal decree and the Council of Regents. No representative institutions were created; no parliament was convened.

The decision reflected both the practical circumstances of the regency — a child king could not negotiate with representative bodies — and the political philosophy of the Galwa court. The Kadim-Galwa faction had never accepted the concessions Iacob V had made to merchants, aristocrats, and the Church. The regency represented a return to the absolutist tradition of the Burawi monarchy, centred on the divine right of the king to rule without constraint.

The western aristocracy, the landed nobility known as the '''[[Hoggaanka|Hoga]]''', supported absolutist governance. They had watched with concern as their counterparts in the east secured a formal role in government through the House of the Hogas. In Galwa, the Hoga lords retained influence through the Council of Regents and personal access to the regent, but they did not possess independent institutional power.

=== The New Laws of 1710 ===

In 1710, the regency promulgated the '''New Laws''' (Burawi: ''Xeerarka Cusub''), a comprehensive legal code that established the administrative framework of the kingdom. The New Laws confirmed the absolute authority of the crown while creating a system of regional administrative courts to manage provincial governance.

The administrative system established by the New Laws comprised two levels. At the upper level, the kingdom was divided into '''fourteen provinces''', each governed by a provincial court presided over by an administrator appointed by the imperial court. At the lower level, each province was subdivided into '''counties''' — 115 counties in total across the kingdom — each managed by a county administrator likewise appointed by the imperial court.

The two-level system centralised appointment power in the regent and her council. All provincial and county administrators served at the pleasure of the crown; no offices were hereditary, and no local bodies possessed independent authority. The administrative courts exercised jurisdiction over taxation, land tenure, criminal justice, and commercial regulation within their territories, but their decisions were subject to appeal to the imperial court at [[Hargiesa]].

The New Laws represented a deliberate departure from the feudal traditions that had characterised the unified Burawi monarchy. Under the old system, Hoga lords had exercised substantial autonomy within their domains, maintaining their own courts, levying their own taxes, and administering justice according to local custom. The New Laws stripped the aristocracy of these prerogatives, converting the Hoga from autonomous lords into courtiers dependent on royal favour.

The regency justified the centralisation as necessary for the new kingdom's survival. A small state, surrounded by potential rivals and burdened with a distant colonial possession, could not afford the inefficiencies and conflicts of feudal jurisdiction. The administrative courts, staffed by officials loyal to the crown rather than to local interests, would ensure coherent governance and reliable revenue collection.

=== Neutrality in the First War of the Isthmus ===

{{main|First War of the Isthmus}}

The [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716) was the first major conflict between [[Illypnia]]n and [[Fosia]]n colonial powers in the New World. The eastern [[Kingdom of Burawa]], bound by alliance to [[Volisania]], committed fully to the war, dispatching naval forces, ground troops, and financial resources to support the Illypnian coalition. [[Lacashe]], the Burawi protectorate in Abyala, likewise contributed forces.

The Kingdom of Galwa, by contrast, declared '''neutrality'''. The regency had only recently consolidated control over the western provinces; the colonial protectorate of [[Bindorama]] required attention; and the kingdom's military and financial resources were limited. Sahra and her council determined that Galwa could not afford to commit to a distant colonial war while the new state remained vulnerable.

Neutrality preserved Galwa's resources but came at a cost. The Volisanian alliance that Burawa had honoured now bound the eastern kingdom more closely to the Illypnian colonial system. Galwa, remaining outside the conflict, forfeited any claim to Volisanian favour or colonial concessions that might have followed from participation. The decision reinforced the divergent trajectories of the two Burawi kingdoms: Burawa aligned with Illypnia; Galwa oriented toward the Aethiopean and the internal Kaftian trade.

=== Relations with the Church ===

The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] played a complex role in Galwa's early period. The Church had initially supported the Galwa claim during the succession crisis, providing theological justification for Cali IV's succession. After the split, however, the Church hierarchy shifted to a neutral position, with the Cardinal of Burawa serving as intermediary between the two kingdoms.

In Galwa, the Church retained its privileged position. The regency confirmed ecclesiastical properties, tax exemptions, and the Church's role in education and moral regulation. The hierarchy in Galwa remained in communion with the Cardinal at Weli Markos and through him with the broader Catholic world.

The Church's neutrality, however, created tension. The Galwa court had hoped for continued partisan support; the Cardinal's shift to an intermediary role denied them that expectation. Sahra and her council accepted the arrangement — they could not afford a schism with the Church — but the relationship remained cooler than either party would have preferred.

=== Relations with the Kingdom of Burawa ===

Relations between the two Burawi kingdoms remained cold but stable throughout the regency period. Neither recognised the other's ruler as the rightful Burawi monarch; both maintained competing claims to legitimacy. But neither sought to overturn the settlement by force.

The [[Cardinal of Burawa]], seated at [[Weli Markos]], served as the primary intermediary. Church channels facilitated communication, mediated disputes, and preserved a sense of shared Burawi Catholic identity across the political divide. Low-level competition persisted — both kingdoms claimed the right to speak for Burawi interests in international fora; both sought advantage in trade negotiations — but open conflict was avoided.

=== Colonial governance and the Bindorama question ===

{{main|Bindorama}}

The colonial protectorate of [[Bindorama]] posed the greatest challenge to the regency. Assigned to Galwa by the settlement of 1709, Bindorama was a frontier colony with a distinctive social structure.

==== Geography and population ====

Bindorama comprised two distinct regions. The '''coastal zone''' was characterised by plantation agriculture, worked by [[Dakari people|Dakari]] and Burawi settlers, indentured servants, and enslaved persons. A small urban elite of Burawi-origin merchants and planters dominated the coastal cities and controlled the export economy. The coastal population was relatively sparse, concentrated in port cities and plantation estates.

The '''mountainous interior''' was inhabited primarily by indigenous peoples who had retained a substantial degree of autonomy throughout the colonial period. The mountains were remote from the centres of colonial power; indigenous communities had negotiated treaties with the colonial administration that preserved their land rights and local governance structures. By the early eighteenth century, a loose balance of power existed between the colonial state and the indigenous polities.

==== Treaty obligations ====

The 1709 settlement had assigned Bindorama to Galwa as a '''protectorate''' with substantial autonomy — an arrangement parallel to that governing [[Lacashe]]'s relationship with the eastern kingdom. Under the treaty terms, Bindorama was to retain internal self-governance while ceding defence and foreign policy to the Galwa crown.

The regency, however, faced fiscal pressures that encouraged a more extractive approach to the protectorate. The split had deprived the western kingdom of the eastern peninsula's tax base and trade revenues; the crown needed new sources of income. Bindorama's plantation economy and mineral resources offered an attractive target.

==== Legislative Assembly controversy ====

Beginning in the 1720s, the regency implemented a series of measures that violated the spirit, and arguably the letter, of the treaty. A '''legislative assembly''' was created for Bindorama, nominally representing the protectorate's interests. In practice, however, the assembly was structured to favour '''Galwa-originated elites''' — settlers who had migrated from the western provinces during the colonial period and maintained ties to the Galwa court.

The assembly granted Galwa lords and their Bindorama allies preferential access to land grants, trade licenses, and administrative positions. The indigenous communities of the mountain interior and the Burawi-loyalist coastal settlers who had remained sympathetic to the eastern kingdom found themselves excluded from the new political order.

The measures provoked immediate opposition. '''Burawi loyalists''' — settlers who had expected to remain under the eastern kingdom's protection and resented being assigned to Galwa — organised as a faction opposed to the new assembly. They made common cause with '''indigenous leaders''' who saw the assembly as a mechanism for encroaching on treaty-protected lands and undermining traditional governance structures.

{{main|Bindorama Civil War}}

By the mid-1730s, the dispute had escalated beyond political conflict. The [[Bindorama Civil War]] began in 1735 and would continue for seven years, ending only in 1742.

== Accession of Cali IV (1740) ==

{{main|Cali IV of Galwa}}

In 1740, Regent Sahra died. '''Cali IV''', who had been an infant at the time of the split and remained a figurehead throughout the regency, assumed direct rule at approximately thirty-one years of age. The accession marked the end of the regency period and the beginning of Cali IV's independent reign.

The new king inherited multiple challenges: the ongoing civil war in Bindorama, fiscal strains from the military commitment, and a court system that had concentrated power in the regent and the Council of Regents rather than the crown. Cali IV moved quickly to assert his authority, dissolving the Council of Regents and replacing it with a personal privy council of trusted advisors.

=== Bindorama Civil War (1735–1742) ===

{{main|Bindorama Civil War}}

The Bindorama Civil War was the defining crisis of Cali IV's early reign. Fought between 1735 and 1742, the conflict pitted '''Burawi loyalists allied with indigenous polities''' against '''Galwa colonial forces and their Bindorama elite collaborators'''.

==== Causes ====

The war arose from Galwa's violation of the 1709 treaty terms. The creation of the legislative assembly, with its preferential treatment of Galwa-aligned elites, had excluded both the Burawi loyalist faction and the indigenous communities of the interior. The loyalists, who had expected autonomy under the protectorate arrangement, saw the assembly as a mechanism for extracting wealth and concentrating power in Galwa hands. The indigenous peoples, whose treaties had guaranteed land rights and self-governance, viewed the assembly as a threat to their traditional order.

In 1735, the excluded factions formed an alliance and launched an armed uprising against the Galwa-aligned administration. The coastal elite and Galwa colonial forces mobilised in response, and the conflict rapidly escalated into full-scale civil war.

==== Course of the war ====

The civil war lasted seven years and devastated Bindorama. The mountainous terrain favoured the loyalist-indigenous alliance, which could rely on knowledge of the landscape and local support networks. The Galwa forces, by contrast, possessed superior resources and access to the coastal ports, but struggled to project power into the interior.

Cali IV, upon his accession in 1740, inherited a war that had ground into a bloody stalemate. The new king committed additional resources to the conflict, seeking a decisive victory that would consolidate Galwa control. The effort proved impossible. By 1742, it was clear that the war could not be won without an expenditure of blood and treasure that would bankrupt the kingdom.

==== Outcome: the creation of Asikyira ====

{{main|Asikyira}}

In 1742, Cali IV negotiated a settlement that ended the civil war. The terms recognised the '''independence of Asikyira''', a new state comprising the mountainous interior and those coastal districts that had supported the loyalist-indigenous alliance.

The creation of Asikyira represented a significant loss for Galwa. The western kingdom had entered the post-split period with one colonial protectorate; it emerged from the civil war having lost the majority of that territory. Asikyira, a mixed Burawi-indigenous state with a distinctive cultural character, became fully independent — neither a Galwa protectorate nor aligned with the eastern kingdom.

== Parliament of Lords (1743) ==

{{main|Parliament of Lords (Galwa)}}

In 1743, following the conclusion of the Bindorama Civil War, Cali IV promulgated the '''[[National Reorganization Edict of 1743]]'''. The edict created the '''[[Parliament of Lords (Galwa)|Parliament of Lords]]''' as a representative assembly of the titled aristocracy and the Church hierarchy, introducing limited representative government to Galwa.

=== Creation of lordships ===

The National Reorganization Edict created a new class of titled nobility, the '''[[Lordships of Galwa]]''', which formed the basis of parliamentary representation. The lordships were deeded to two groups:

The first group comprised '''relatives of the Galwa branch''' of the Kadim dynasty — cousins, uncles, and cadet branches that had supported Sahra's regency and Cali IV's accession. These family members had expected rewards for their loyalty; the lordships provided them with permanent status and a role in governance.

The second group consisted of '''repatriated elites from the Bindorama Civil War'''. The Galwa-aligned settlers and administrators who had fled Bindorama following the creation of Asikyira had lost their lands, positions, and wealth. The edict compensated them with lordships in the mainland kingdom, converting colonial losses into domestic titles. The repatriated elites brought with them administrative experience, military credentials from the civil war, and a deep resentment of the Burawi loyalists and indigenous peoples who had driven them from Bindorama.

=== The Parliament's composition and powers ===

The Parliament of Lords was an advisory and legislative body, empowered to deliberate on matters of taxation, inheritance, and land tenure. The Church hierarchy, represented by bishops and abbots, sat alongside the secular lords. The parliament was subordinate to royal authority in matters of defence, foreign policy, and ecclesiastical relations — the crown retained decisive prerogatives.

The reform represented a departure from the absolutist tradition of the regency period. Cali IV, having witnessed the gridlock that plagued Burawa's four-house parliament, sought to create a simpler structure that would incorporate aristocratic and ecclesiastical interests without paralysing the crown. The parliament would provide a forum for elite concerns; the crown would preserve the authority to act.

=== Political purpose ===

The creation of the Parliament of Lords served multiple purposes. It rewarded the Galwa branch for its loyalty and compensated the repatriated Bindorama elites for their losses. It incorporated the aristocracy into a formal governing institution after decades of absolutist centralisation under the New Laws. And it provided Cali IV with a body of supporters whose interests were now aligned with the stability and success of the Galwa kingdom, rather than with dreams of reconquering Bindorama.

The Parliament of Lords marked the end of the western kingdom had early Kingdom of Galwa period. The passed through the challenges of the regency, the accession of the adult king, and the colonial crisis; it emerged with a political system that, while more limited than the eastern kingdom's parliament, nonetheless represented a move toward representative governance.

== Legacy ==

The early Kingdom of Galwa period established the distinctive trajectory of the western Burawi kingdom. Following the 1709 split, Galwa developed institutions that differed from its eastern counterpart: absolutist governance under the regency, followed by limited aristocratic representation under the adult king. The kingdom's neutrality in the First War of the Isthmus preserved resources but foreclosed colonial opportunities. The Bindorama Civil War and the creation of Asikyira demonstrated the limits of Galwa's capacity to project power and the difficulty of governing a distant protectorate with a resistant population.

The Church's intermediary role, formalised in the settlement of 1709, persisted throughout the period. The Cardinal of Burawa at Weli Markos mediated disputes, preserved ecclesiastical unity, and facilitated communication between the two kingdoms. The arrangement would continue to shape relations between the Burawi states for decades to come.

